# Transcripts are committed GZIPPED (`*.jsonl.gz`) — they compress 12-21x, so
# the full record of every run is under 1 MB instead of 16 MB. `metrics.iter_jsonl`
# and `verify.py` read either form, so the audit path works directly on the
# committed files with no unzip step:
#
#   python -m experiments.llm_eval.verify --deep
#
# The uncompressed working copies a run produces are ignored; the archive is not.
# Transcripts are NOT regenerable — they contain real model responses, which are
# not deterministic — so the .gz files are the record, not a cache.
*.jsonl
estimate/

# Per-run directories (`runs/<UTC stamp>/`, see layout.py). Their `summary.json`
# IS tracked and deliberately so: it is the only record of what one invocation
# spent and how far it got, and losing it is the defect the per-run layout
# exists to fix — a twelve-invocation session left eleven summaries overwritten
# and its cost accounting had to be rebuilt by summing transcripts by hand.
#
# Their transcripts are covered by `*.jsonl` above: a run's working copies are
# scratch until promoted into the archive, which is a deliberate act (gzip,
# commit) because promotion asserts the data backs a published number.
runs/*/figure.png
